<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Jingnan</h1><p class="st-meta">924 CE – 963 CE · capital Jiangling</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The smallest of the Ten Kingdoms, a cluster of prefectures around Jiangling on the Yangtze that survived for four decades by bowing to every larger neighbour.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-jixing">Gao Jixing</a>, a former slave adopted into a household close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a>, was appointed military governor of Jingnan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a> in 907. After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a> took power he was made Prince of Nanping in 924, and his family ruled the region as hereditary lords.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With only three prefectures, Jingnan lived by diplomacy and commerce. It sat at a crossroads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> river trade, and its rulers accepted titles from the northern dynasties and from southern states such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wu-ten-kingdoms">Wu (Ten Kingdoms)</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-shu">Later Shu</a> at the same time. Other courts mocked them as '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> the Shameless' for seizing passing tribute and then apologising, but the policy kept the state alive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 963 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> troops, sent through Jingnan on a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-jichong">Gao Jichong</a> surrendered without fighting. His family was treated generously, and Jingnan's fall was the first step in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> conquest of the southern kingdoms.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jingnan.jpg" alt="Jingnan" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Tang.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>924 CE – 963 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiangling</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gao Jixing, Gao Conghui, Gao Jichong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nanping, Northern Chu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=944&amp;focus=jingnan" aria-label="Jingnan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7600 1081 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jingnan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7920 1337L7898 1125M7920 1337L7901 1174"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7898 1125h0M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7920 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7920 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=944&amp;focus=jingnan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

